    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d470">

                <pb facs="0396" n="390" xml:id="pg390"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1163Am3/411: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Lockhart</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-10-07T11:00:00+08:00">October 7, 1939—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received October 7—9 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>531. Peiping’s 509, September 25, 5 p.m. and 523, October 3, 6 p.m.<anchor n="68"
                        xml:id="d470-f1"/></p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Representative of Sisters of Providence, just arrived in Peiping, has
                        informed Embassy that, in addition to the demand on the part of the Honan
                        Provincial Bureau of Education that their school accept a Japanese adviser,
                        the authorities now demand that the mission deposit with them the sum of
                        60,000 Chinese dollars, presumably in the nature of a guarantee.
                        Representative states school has voluntarily complied with all requirements
                        for registration except the two mentioned above and that the sisters would
                        prefer to close the school rather than submit to these terms.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Embassy believes that further representations to Japanese Embassy here,
                        based on the new developments, would be advisable. It is felt that the
                        requirement that the school deposit funds with the provincial authorities is
                        particularly obnoxious and, if acceded to, might set a most unfortunate
                        precedent. The Department’s instructions are respectfully requested.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>Embassy feels that representations to the Japanese Foreign Office might
                        materially assist in reaching an expeditious and satisfactory settlement of
                        this case and respectfully suggests that the Department may wish to consider
                        instructing the Embassy at Tokyo to that effect.</item>
                </list>
                <p>Repeated to Chungking, Hankow and Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lockhart</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
